# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SnZ Ventures — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local for development, or paste into Vercel → Settings →
# Environment Variables for production. Fill in ONLY what you actually use.
#
# Never commit .env.local. This template is the only env file in git, and it
# must never contain a real value.
#
# Design rule throughout the app: a missing variable degrades the feature
# honestly (503 + a human explanation). It never falls back to a guessable
# default and it never pretends the feature worked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Canonical origin. Drives canonical URLs, OG tags, sitemap, robots and the
# password-reset links sent by email.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.snzventures.com

# ⚠ DEPLOYMENT REGION — see vercel.json.
#
# Functions are pinned to dub1 (Dublin) because the Supabase database is in
# eu-west-1. A function in Singapore talking to a database in Ireland paid a
# full round trip per query and hit FUNCTION_INVOCATION_TIMEOUT. If the database
# ever moves, change `regions` in vercel.json to match it.

# Where the client portal is served from. LEAVE BLANK while the portal runs as
# part of this application (/login, /register, /portal) — that is the default
# and it works with no configuration.
#
# Set it to an ORIGIN ONLY (no trailing path) once the portal is deployed to
# its own subdomain, and every portal CTA on the public site re-points at once:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL=https://student.snzventures.com
#
# Do NOT set this to a marketing site. These links must land on a page that can
# actually sign a client in.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL=

# Serve THIS deployment as the client portal and nothing else: `/` becomes the
# sign-in screen and the marketing pages are unreachable. Leave blank for the
# public website.
#
# Locally you do not need to set this by hand — `npm run dev:portal` sets it
# and runs the portal on http://localhost:3001 while the public site keeps
# running on 3000 from the same codebase.
#
# Set it to 1 on the portal subdomain deployment in production. It is a ROUTING
# mode, not a security boundary: every server-side guard runs regardless.
PORTAL_ONLY=

# === ROLE PREVIEW / DEMO MODE (development only) ===========================
# Switches on /demo — four sign-in-free previews of the portal (Super Admin,
# Student, Job Seeker, Business) built entirely from sample data in
# lib/demo/data.ts. Nothing in it touches the database or the session system,
# and real sign-in is unaffected.
#
# Locally you do not need to set this by hand — `npm run dev:demo` sets it and
# serves the preview on http://localhost:3002.
#
# NEVER SET THIS IN PRODUCTION. As a second barrier the code refuses to enable
# it whenever VERCEL_ENV is "production", regardless of this value — so a
# careless paste into a production dashboard does nothing.
#
# To remove the feature entirely: delete app/demo/, lib/demo/,
# components/demo/, and this variable. No production file references them.
DEMO_MODE=


# === CLIENT PORTAL — DATABASE (required to enable accounts) ================
# Any PostgreSQL 13+ instance: Neon, Supabase, Vercel Postgres, RDS, Railway.
# No extensions are required — the schema uses core gen_random_uuid() only,
# so it installs on managed roles that cannot CREATE EXTENSION.
#
# Use the POOLED connection string on serverless (Neon "-pooler", Supabase
# port 6543). The client runs with prepare:false so transaction pooling is safe.
#
#   postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require
#
# Then, once: npm run db:migrate && npm run db:bootstrap -- --email you@…
DATABASE_URL=


# === CLIENT PORTAL — SESSION SIGNING (required) ============================
# Signs session cookies and password-reset tokens. 32+ characters.
# Without it the portal refuses to authenticate anyone rather than falling
# back to a predictable key. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Generate it OUTSIDE the repository and paste it straight into Vercel.
# It must never appear in source, in git, or in a log line.
AUTH_SECRET=


# === GOOGLE SIGN-IN (optional) =============================================
# Adds "Continue with Google" to the portal login. WITHOUT BOTH VALUES the
# button is not rendered at all and the routes return a clear message — the
# feature is absent rather than broken.
#
# To switch it on:
#   1. Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials
#   2. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID, type "Web application"
#   3. Authorised redirect URI — must match EXACTLY, including scheme:
#        https://www.snzventures.com/api/auth/google/callback
#      Add a second entry for local work if needed:
#        http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#   4. Configure the OAuth consent screen (app name, support email, logo)
#
# The redirect URI is derived from NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL, so that variable must
# be correct for the environment or Google will reject the callback.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is read only inside a `server-only` module and is never
# sent to the browser. Do not add a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix to it.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=


# === DOCUMENT STORAGE ======================================================
# Client documents contain passports, transcripts and financial records.
# They are NEVER written to /public, to git, or to the Vercel filesystem.
# Configure ONE transport. Without one, uploads return 503.

# Option A (RECOMMENDED HERE) — Supabase Storage.
# This project already uses Supabase for the database, so this needs no new
# vendor: two values from the dashboard you already have open. The bucket is
# created PRIVATE on first upload and every download is a server-signed,
# short-lived URL. Nothing is ever public.
#
# ⚠ The service_role key bypasses row-level security. It is read only inside
# lib/storage.ts, which is imported solely by API routes, and must NEVER be
# given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# BOTH of the next two are required. storageTransport() in lib/storage.ts only
# selects Supabase when it can see the pair, so setting the key alone leaves
# the transport at "none" and uploads answering 503 with nothing to explain it.
#
# SUPABASE_URL is NOT derivable from DATABASE_URL — that one is a postgres://
# pooler string, this is the project's HTTPS API origin. They are different
# values from different panels and both have to be pasted.
#
#   Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → Project URL
#     https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=
#   Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role key
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# Optional — defaults to "client-documents".
SUPABASE_DOCUMENTS_BUCKET=


# Option B — S3-compatible, PRIVATE bucket.
# Works with AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, MinIO.
# The bucket must block all public access; the app mints short-lived
# (~2 minute) signed GET URLs per authorised download.
S3_BUCKET=
S3_REGION=auto
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Option B — Vercel Blob. Simpler to provision, but note the honest caveat:
# the Blob SDK only issues `access: "public"` objects, so the unguessable
# 20-hex key is the only boundary. Access control still happens server-side
# (the key is never sent to a browser), but prefer S3 for regulated data.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
BLOB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=


# === EMAIL DELIVERY ========================================================
# Enquiry forms, email verification and password resets all use this.
# Configure ONE transport. Without one, the enquiry API returns 503 and the
# form shows the direct contact details — it never pretends a message was sent.

# Option A — Resend (https://resend.com)
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Option B — any webhook (Zapier, Make, n8n, a CRM intake, an SMTP relay)
MAIL_WEBHOOK_URL=
MAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Sender must be on a domain you control and have verified with the provider.
MAIL_FROM="SnZ Ventures <noreply@snzventures.com>"
# Where enquiries land. Defaults to info@snzventures.com.
MAIL_TO=info@snzventures.com


# === GOOGLE REVIEWS ========================================================
# Pulls the real reviews from the Google Business listing, server-side, once
# per day. Without these the reviews section shows an honest placeholder and
# links visitors to the public listing — it never invents a review.
#
# The key is billable and must NEVER be exposed to the browser, so it has no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and lib/reviews.ts is marked `server-only`. Restrict the
# key to the Places API in the Google Cloud console.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=
# OPTIONAL. Left blank, the listing is resolved by name and office address via
# Text Search using the same key, so the API key alone is enough to switch the
# section on. Set it to pin an exact listing and skip that lookup — it is the
# Place ID (`ChIJ…`), not the share link.
GOOGLE_PLACE_ID=


# === ANALYTICS & TRACKING ==================================================
# ⚠ Implement cookie consent BEFORE setting any of these for EU visitors.
# Nothing loads while these are blank.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GADS_ID=


# === ASKING THE PORTAL QUESTIONS FROM CLAUDE (MCP) =========================
# Turns on /api/mcp, a read-only endpoint that lets Claude answer questions
# about the portal — "who has not sent a passport scan", "every Feb 2027
# applicant's passport number and date of birth" — instead of somebody opening
# the admin area and copying by hand.
#
# BLANK MEANS OFF. The endpoint returns 503 until this is set; it never falls
# back to open. Set it to a long random string and nothing else:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Whoever holds this can read every client's file, so treat it exactly like a
# password: paste it straight into the Vercel dashboard, never into a chat,
# a commit, or a screenshot. Replacing it here revokes every existing client.
#
# Every call is written to audit_logs as `mcp.read` with the tool and its
# arguments, so what was read and when is on the record.
MCP_TOKEN=
